{% extends "doc_page.jinja" %}
{% import "chrome.jinja" as chrome %}
{% import "components.jinja" as ui %}
{% import "forthcoming_data.jinja" as fc %}
{% block page_title %}Owned Artefacts (Forthcoming) - Netsuke Build System{% endblock %}
{% block content %}

    <div class="site-container pt-16 grow flex">

        {{ fc.sidebar('artefacts') }}

        <!-- Main Content -->
        <main id="main-content" class="hm-docs-content flex-1 min-w-0 py-12 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-12">

            {% call fc.header('artefacts') %}
                    Every repository grows a cleanup list, and every cleanup list drifts away from whatever produces the files. RFC 0023 proposes
                    optional named artefacts: exact paths a manifest claims to own, and one bounded cleanup operation that previews its scope,
                    asks for consent, and deletes nothing it has not validated.
            {% endcall %}

            <section id="status" class="mb-16">
                <div class="bg-warning/5 border border-warning/30 rounded-xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="font-bold text-base-content text-sm uppercase tracking-wide mb-3 flex items-center">
                        <span class="iconify text-warning mr-2" data-icon="carbon:information-filled" aria-hidden="true"></span> Status on {{ generated_at.day }} {{ generated_at.strftime('%B %Y') }}
                    </h2>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-accent mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:close" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Not in v{{ netsuke_version }}.</strong> The released compiler has no <code>artefacts</code> mapping, <code>produces</code> field, or <code>clean_owned</code> operation, and <code>netsuke clean</code> has no <code>--artefact</code> option.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-success mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:document" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Proposed.</strong> <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0023-artefact-ownership-and-scoped-cleanup.md" class="hm-link">RFC 0023</a> has status Proposed, created 19 September 2026, and landed in the documentation-only <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/commit/54837e9" class="hm-link">commit 54837e9</a>.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-warning mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:task" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Planned as phase 24.</strong> The <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap-progressive-enhancement.md#24-owned-artefacts-and-bounded-cleanup" class="hm-link">progressive-enhancement roadmap</a> splits the work into six tasks; none is ticked.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:tag" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>No release named.</strong> Supported-platform deletion primitives, resource-lease identity, ownership and source conflict rules, and concrete operator limits are to be ratified before implementation.</span></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="why" class="mb-16">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:idea" aria-hidden="true"></span> Why ownership
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Today <code>netsuke clean</code> runs <code>ninja -t clean</code>, which removes the file outputs Ninja tracks and nothing else
                    (see <a href="/netsuke/docs/cli/#clean-maintenance" class="hm-link">the CLI reference</a>). Anything an action writes &mdash; a
                    benchmark report, a cache, a prepared environment &mdash; falls outside it, so projects reach for a shell recipe, and
                    &ldquo;shell fragments obscure path, symlink, and error semantics.&rdquo;
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Different retention decisions</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">A report, a shared tool cache, a temporary directory, and a prepared environment should not share one blunt deletion rule.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Existing owners stay put</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Ninja keeps ordinary output cleaning, the runtime keeps its dyndep sidecars, and RFC 0010 keeps command-local temporary directories. Nothing infers ownership from command text.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">An assertion, not a proof</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">&ldquo;Ownership is an assertion by the manifest author, not proof that a directory contains no valuable files.&rdquo; The design makes scope inspectable and enforces its bounds without claiming to sandbox recipes.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="declaring" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:box" aria-hidden="true"></span> Declaring ownership
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    An artefact has a required <code>path</code> and optional <code>kind</code>, <code>role</code>, and <code>create_parent</code>. The
                    smallest declaration owns one directory and needs nothing else &mdash; no state, typed input, context, or package provider.
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
                    <div class="min-w-0">
                        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-charcoal-mid mb-2">Proposed syntax, RFC 0023 &sect;3 (verbatim)</p>
                        <div class="bg-base-content rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size]" tabindex="0">
{% highlight 'netsuke' %}{% raw %}
artefacts:
  build-output:
    path: build
    kind: directory

actions:
  - name: clean-build
    command:
      clean_owned: [build-output]
{% endraw %}{% endhighlight %}
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="min-w-0">
                        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-charcoal-mid mb-2">Proposed syntax, RFC 0023 &sect;3 (verbatim; the command is illustrative)</p>
                        <div class="bg-base-content rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size]" tabindex="0">
{% highlight 'netsuke' %}{% raw %}
artefacts:
  benchmark-report:
    path: dist/benchmarks/microbenchmarks.json
    role: report
    create_parent: true

actions:
  - name: benchmark
    produces: [benchmark-report]
    command:
      invoke: python benchmarks/run.py --output dist/benchmarks/microbenchmarks.json
{% endraw %}{% endhighlight %}
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid mb-8 max-w-3xl">
                    <code>produces</code> &ldquo;records ownership and expected outputs; it does not make a benchmark incremental or authorize replay
                    of an old result.&rdquo; A declared output that is missing after the producer succeeds is a producer failure. Partial outputs from a
                    failed run are kept for inspection, never quietly removed or reused as evidence.
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Exact paths</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid"><code>path</code> names exactly one workspace-relative object. <code>kind</code> defaults to <code>file</code>; recursive ownership needs an explicit <code>directory</code>. No globs in the first version.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">A directory claims its subtree</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Including children created later. The preview must say so plainly, and the claim is unsuitable for a directory shared with source files or another owner's mutable state.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">One owner, one producer</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Overlapping or case-equivalent roots are rejected, as are multiple producers. Roles &mdash; <code>generated</code> (default), <code>report</code>, <code>cache</code>, <code>environment</code> &mdash; describe intent, not a deletion or caching rule.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="cleanup" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:clean" aria-hidden="true"></span> Scoped cleanup
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Cleanup has two front doors and one implementation. In a recipe, <code>clean_owned: [NAME, ...]</code> is an execution unit in the
                    <a href="{{ fc.href('structured-commands') }}" class="hm-link">structured command</a> union; it names artefacts only and
                    &ldquo;never accepts raw shell paths.&rdquo; On the command line, <code>clean</code> gains a repeatable <code>--artefact NAME</code>.
                    Both share the same planner, validator, deleter, and structured results.
                </p>
                <div class="bg-base-content rounded-xl overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size] mb-6" tabindex="0">
                    <div class="bg-charcoal-mid/20 px-4 py-2 border-b border-white/10 text-xs font-mono text-stone-light">Proposed commands, RFC 0023 &sect;5 (verbatim)</div>
                    {% highlight 'netsuke-console' %}
$ netsuke clean --artefact build-output --dry-run
$ netsuke clean --artefact build-output --force --no-input
{% endhighlight %}
                </div>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Preview</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">A dry-run lists normalized roots, recursive ownership, existing and missing objects, retained siblings, and any rejected scope. It is &ldquo;a read-only plan, not a reusable authorization token&rdquo;: execution resolves and validates again. A plan that exceeds its bounds fails rather than truncating.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Consent</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">The existing <code>--dry-run</code>, <code>--force</code>, and <code>--no-input</code> flags; no cleanup-specific confirmation framework. A noninteractive destructive run without explicit consent fails before deleting. &ldquo;<code>--force</code> skips confirmation, not validation, ownership conflicts, or bounds.&rdquo;</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Protected scope</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Paths anchor at the workspace capability. Absolute paths, empty paths, the workspace root, parent traversal, and platform escape forms are rejected. The manifest, loaded configuration, known runtime scripts, declared sources, <code>.git</code>, and Netsuke's runtime directories are protected; a directory claim containing one is invalid.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Filesystem safety</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Never follow a symlink or Windows reparse point during traversal; no lexical <code>starts_with</code> checks and no ambient <code>rm -rf</code>. Removing a hard link never truncates the shared file. Exceeding a bound in the admission pass causes zero deletions.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-primary/5 border border-primary/20 rounded-lg p-5 flex items-start">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-3" data-icon="carbon:information" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">
                        <strong>Plain <code>clean</code> is unchanged.</strong> Without <code>--artefact</code>, <code>netsuke clean</code> keeps its Ninja-output
                        behaviour. With it, only the selected roots are cleaned: no Ninja outputs, environments, caches, or runtime directories join
                        implicitly. Roadmap: <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap-progressive-enhancement.md#242-delete-only-validated-scope-through-one-implementation" class="hm-link">24.2</a>.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="lifecycle" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:renew" aria-hidden="true"></span> Lifecycle and migration
                </h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-8">
                    <div class="min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">Environments and states</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid">
                            An <code>environment</code> artefact may name the same path as a <a href="{{ fc.href('states', 'evidence') }}" class="hm-link">managed state</a>.
                            When it is selected for cleanup, Netsuke acquires the state's integrity lease and invalidates its readiness records before any
                            deletion, failure paths included. &ldquo;Probe success from before cleanup cannot establish subsequent readiness.&rdquo; Neither
                            feature requires the other: the artefact-only path never demands a state declaration.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">Leases and ordering</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid">
                            Cleanup takes an exclusive lease over each selected root, ordered canonically, sharing the state-resource identity boundary
                            rather than a second lock system. A selected build that both cleans and produces or consumes the same resource is rejected:
                            &ldquo;A Ninja pool would serialize access but would not establish the user's intended order.&rdquo; Separate invocations are the
                            remedy.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Migrating Cuprum</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Declare disjoint output roots first, and keep unmatched wildcard cleanup as an ordinary command until a separate safe file-selection contract exists. Unrestricted glob deletion is explicitly not added just to reproduce a long shell command.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Not delivery</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">No remote delivery, content-addressed store, or provenance attestation. The main roadmap's <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md#54-delivery-and-feedback" class="hm-link">delivery task 5.4.2</a> waits for ownership to be modelled; this RFC models it and stops there.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mt-6">Roadmap: <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap-progressive-enhancement.md#243-integrate-lifecycle-invalidation-and-staged-migration" class="hm-link">24.3, lifecycle invalidation and staged migration</a>.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="shallow-end" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:flow" aria-hidden="true"></span> From the shallow end
                </h2>
                <div class="bg-primary/5 border border-primary/20 rounded-lg p-5 flex items-start mb-8">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-3" data-icon="carbon:security" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid">
                        <strong>The guarantee belongs to <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0025-progressive-enhancement-and-maturity-policies.md#2-the-shallow-end-contract" class="hm-link">RFC 0025</a>.</strong>
                        No heuristic promotes a redirection to an ownership claim. Across the proposal set, unused state or context declarations never cause runtime probes,
                        installation, network access, or resource acquisition. The quickstart manifest stays a release-acceptance fixture, and basic usage
                        is &ldquo;a supported destination, not a temporary migration stage.&rdquo; RFC 0023 calls itself &ldquo;an opt-in improvement rather than
                        an onboarding prerequisite.&rdquo;
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-8">
                    <div class="min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">What stays optional</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-success mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>Plain commands, existing targets, and existing <code>clean</code> behaviour remain supported; the hello-world and legacy <code>clean</code> fixtures stay unchanged.</span></li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-success mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>No target needs an artefact declaration. &ldquo;Requiring an artefact declaration for every existing target would make an optional benefit contagious.&rdquo;</span></li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-success mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>A declaration with no producer is still useful for a legacy build directory: declaration, bounded preview, and authorization are enough.</span></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">The route in, one step at a time</h3>
                        <ol class="space-y-3 text-sm text-charcoal-mid list-decimal pl-5">
                            <li>Keep <code>netsuke clean</code> for Ninja outputs.</li>
                            <li>Declare one owned directory with only <code>path</code> and <code>kind</code>, and clean it with <code>clean_owned</code>.</li>
                            <li>Preview with <code>clean --artefact NAME --dry-run</code> before trusting it.</li>
                            <li>Name producers with <code>produces</code> for reports and other action outputs.</li>
                            <li>Tie an <code>environment</code> artefact to a <a href="{{ fc.href('states', 'declaring') }}" class="hm-link">state</a>, if the project uses states.</li>
                            <li>Opt in to the <code>owned-cleanup</code> <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0025-progressive-enhancement-and-maturity-policies.md#5-initial-rule-contracts" class="hm-link">maturity rule</a> for selected cleanup actions.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Roadmap <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap-progressive-enhancement.md#241-establish-ownership-before-permitting-deletion" class="hm-link">24.1</a> establishes ownership before any deletion is permitted, and task 24.3.2 must &ldquo;demonstrate that the one-directory feature works without state syntax.&rdquo;</p>
            </section>

            <section id="confidence" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:certificate-check" aria-hidden="true"></span> Confidence
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Planned, not existing. RFC 0023 &sect;8 and roadmap phase 24 set these acceptance requirements for code that deletes files.
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Properties</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Canonical-path uniqueness, scope normalization, protected roots, and duplicate selection.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Adversarial end to end</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Symlinks, reparse points, root replacement, hard links, permission errors, case collisions, interrupted partial deletion, entry-budget overflow, and missing paths. External sentinel files must remain unchanged.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Previews that write nothing</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Zero filesystem writes in dry-run, including no preparation or probe execution; refusal without consent; fresh validation on replay; and bounds that <code>--force</code> cannot bypass.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl">
                    The design refuses claims it cannot back. &ldquo;No claim of race freedom is acceptable until adversarial replacement tests pass on each
                    supported platform.&rdquo; Cleanup &ldquo;is not an atomic transaction and must never report rollback that it did not perform.&rdquo; Without
                    source-control metadata Netsuke cannot identify every source file, and the contract must say so. A later <code>cargo clean</code>
                    recipe keeps Cargo's semantics, outside Netsuke's guarantee.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="access" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:accessibility" aria-hidden="true"></span> Accessibility and localization
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Every human-readable message Netsuke emits is localized by default through its <a href="/netsuke/docs/cli/#localization" class="hm-link">Fluent catalogues</a>, and cleanup is no
                    exception: previews, confirmations, and failure reports arrive in the operator's locale, while artefact names, paths, and
                    reason codes stay exactly as written so they can be matched. RFC 0023 adds requirements that bear directly on anyone
                    deciding, from text alone, whether to delete something, and they apply in Netsuke's
                    <a href="/netsuke/docs/cli/#accessible-output" class="hm-link">accessible output mode</a> as much as anywhere:
                </p>
                <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl mb-6">
                    <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>The preview must state recursive scope &ldquo;plainly&rdquo;, and an interactive run asks for confirmation only after displaying it.</span></li>
                    <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>Partial failures report exact removed, retained, and failed objects rather than a summary verdict.</span></li>
                    <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>Command and recipe forms return the same structured results, so machine-readable output does not depend on which door was used.</span></li>
                    <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0025-progressive-enhancement-and-maturity-policies.md#8-evaluation-and-diagnostics" class="hm-link">RFC 0025 &sect;8</a> tells maturity diagnostics, including <code>owned-cleanup</code> findings, to &ldquo;Reuse Fluent localization&rdquo;, the machinery behind Netsuke's existing <a href="/netsuke/docs/cli/#localization" class="hm-link">locale catalogues</a>.</span></li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl">Task 24.3.2 documents ownership assertions, recursive scope, retained caches, preview limitations, and external-command boundaries. RFC 0023 does not discuss accessibility beyond these requirements.</p>
            </section>

{{ fc.footer('artefacts') }}

        </main>
    </div>

{% endblock %}
